{
  "families": [
    {
      "budget_share": 0.30,
      "family": "pipelined_m",
      "ranges": [
        {"choices": ["trunc", "round"], "high": null, "low": null, "param": "rounding"},
        {"choices": null, "high": 20, "low": 12, "param": "data_width"},
        {"choices": null, "high": 20, "low": 10, "param": "n_iter"},
        {"choices": null, "high": 4, "low": -2, "param": "angle_guard"},
        {"choices": null, "high": 4, "low": 0, "param": "frac_guard"},
        {"choices": null, "high": 8, "low": 2, "param": "m"}
      ],
      "why": "Pipelined/pipelined_m issue 1 result/cycle, so 50 MSPS only needs ~50 MHz clock — the easiest way to satisfy the throughput constraint while staying at accuracy. pipelined_m lets the FSM trade FFs for combinational depth via m, which is the most likely lever on power_index; searching m over its full 2..8 range here is essential because the lowest-power point will almost certainly sit on this axis."
    },
    {
      "budget_share": 0.30,
      "family": "unrolled_k",
      "ranges": [
        {"choices": ["trunc", "round"], "high": null, "low": null, "param": "rounding"},
        {"choices": null, "high": 20, "low": 12, "param": "data_width"},
        {"choices": null, "high": 22, "low": 10, "param": "n_iter"},
        {"choices": null, "high": 4, "low": -2, "param": "angle_guard"},
        {"choices": null, "high": 4, "low": 0, "param": "frac_guard"},
        {"choices": null, "high": 8, "low": 2, "param": "k"}
      ],
      "why": "Uses a shared datapath (no per-stage pipeline registers), so it can be much smaller than a full pipeline, but it must run fast enough that Fmax/(ceil(N/k)+3) >= 50. The k vs N/k trade-off decides both the required clock and the depth, so I want the whole k=2..8 sweep plus a wide N range to find the low-power/high-accuracy corner."
    },
    {
      "budget_share": 0.30,
      "family": "pipelined",
      "ranges": [
        {"choices": ["trunc", "round"], "high": null, "low": null, "param": "rounding"},
        {"choices": null, "high": 20, "low": 12, "param": "data_width"},
        {"choices": null, "high": 22, "low": 10, "param": "n_iter"},
        {"choices": null, "high": 4, "low": -2, "param": "angle_guard"},
        {"choices": null, "high": 4, "low": 0, "param": "frac_guard"}
      ],
      "why": "The clean full-pipeline reference: guaranteed 1 result/cycle (so throughput is trivially met), maximal Fmax and lowest latency, at the cost of N stages of registers. It anchors the power/accuracy front and gives the m=1 baseline that pipelined_m must beat; keeping a full share here guarantees coverage of the low-latency / high-accuracy end."
    },
    {
      "budget_share": 0.10,
      "family": "iterative",
      "ranges": [
        {"choices": ["trunc", "round"], "high": null, "low": null, "param": "rounding"},
        {"choices": null, "high": 20, "low": 12, "param": "data_width"},
        {"choices": null, "high": 20, "low": 8, "param": "n_iter"},
        {"choices": null, "high": 4, "low": -2, "param": "angle_guard"},
        {"choices": null, "high": 4, "low": 0, "param": "frac_guard"}
      ],
      "why": "The reference topology and per-cycle-parsimonious datapath; its throughput is Fmax/(N+3), so reaching 50 MSPS needs ~50*(N+3) MHz which is implausible at the typical corner for the N>=12 accuracy needs — I expect it to be infeasible. A small share is worth spending to empirically confirm it (or catch a lucky high-Fmax point) before excluding it, since if feasible it would likely be the power winner."
    }
  ],
  "rationale": "Only pipelined/pipelined_m and (with a high enough clock) unrolled_k can plausibly hit 50 MSPS at >=12-bit accuracy; iterative almost certainly cannot and gets only a token share to prove it. Accuracy needs only ~2^-12, so the useful data_width/n_iter region is modest, but because the HV objective rewards accuracy above the 12-bit reference the widths/iterations are allowed up to ~20 to map the upper part of the front. Round 1 is intentionally broad over all four families and wide parameter ranges (especially the k and m axes and the guards, which are the main power/accuracy levers), weighting the three feasible families equally so the NSGA-II sees the whole trade-off curve before later rounds zoom in on the surviving families."
}

{
  "decision": "map_front",
  "next_plan": null,
  "rationale": "The front is real but covers only a sliver of one objective's range, and the HV maths says that sliver is where the remaining gains are. Evidence: (1) Only two families can ever be feasible — iterative topped out at 11 MSPS and unrolled_k at 7.14 MSPS against the hard 50 MSPS constraint, so 0/10 and 0/30 feasible respectively; the front is entirely pipelined + pipelined_m. (2) The front has 4 unique points spanning power_index 2.85..5.42 against an HV reference of 40, and accuracy_bits 13.4..15.5 against a reference of 12, while round 1 already saw 16.22 bits anywhere and the registry permits data_width=28 / n_iter=30. (3) Decomposing HV at ref (40,12): the band p=5.42..40 at a=15.55 alone contributes ~(40-5.42)*(15.55-12)=122.8 of the 129.1 total, so ~95% of the score is the 'high-accuracy tail' — every extra accuracy bit is worth ~34 HV, while squeezing power below 2.85 (e.g. to 2.0 at 13.4 bits) is worth <2 HV. (4) Refining around 2.85 would mostly re-find the point the selection rule already picks; the untapped budget is in accuracy/power combinations that round 1's 30-eval-per-family sampling never visited (pipelined feasible ranges were only data_width 17..20, pipelined_m only m=2). Therefore let code run NSGA-II over the FULL registry ranges of the two feasible families (pipelined, pipelined_m), seeded with the current front, to fill the accuracy_bits 15.5..~20 and power_index 2.5..20 region before the reserved end-of-run mapping."
}

{
  "rationale": "All four families are now explored, so the choice is where to spend the remaining 200 evals. Evidence: (1) iterative (0/10 feasible, max 11 MSPS) and unrolled_k (0/30 feasible, max 7.14 MSPS) cannot reach the 50 MSPS constraint at all — they are dead ends and should get no more budget. (2) The entire 10-point front is pipelined_m with m=2; pipelined's best feasible power_index (3.93) is worse than pipelined_m's (2.85) and its best accuracy (20.80) is below pipelined_m's (22.84), so pipelined_m dominates and is the family to refine. (3) The front's low end stops at accuracy_bits=13.4 (power 2.85), but the spec only requires max_abs_err <= 2^-12, i.e. 12 bits — the whole 12.0..13.4 accuracy band is unmapped. Because the HV reference power is 40, each accuracy bit added near power~2.5-2.85 is worth ~(40-2.85)≈37 of HV, so filling this low-power/low-accuracy corner is the single largest HV opportunity. (4) The high end stops at 22.7 bits (power 6.96) while the registry allows data_width up to 28 and n_iter up to 30, so a modest extension is possible. (5) m is only 2 on the front even though m=2 still leaves throughput headroom (72.3 MSPS at data_width=17 vs the 50 MSPS floor), so m=3 is worth probing for extra power savings. This is a targeted refine; the reserved final round can then map the full pipelined_m ranges.",
