  ,"rationale": "HV is 380 and only +0.5% this round, and its shape says where the remaining headroom is. With reference (power=40, accuracy=12), the single top point (6.96, 22.74) contributes about (40-6.96)*(22.74-12) ~ 353 of the 380, i.e. ~93% of the hypervolume; the low-power corner contributes almost nothing because every feasible design sits at accuracy >= 12 by constraint. So the corner (high data_width, high n_iter, round + larger guards) is where both coverage and the staircase improve. Evidence that it is not saturated: the top points still use rounding=trunc, angle_guard=2, frac_guard=2, while pipelined_m has already produced 22.84 bits somewhere in its 166 evals but that point is not on the feasible front (it must violate throughput), so the binding limit is fmax>=50 MSPS, not the error knobs - exactly the region to re-search with round + ag/fg up to 4 + n_iter up to 30, and with plain pipelined (max throughput seen 163 MSPS) as the safety valve at data_width 26..28. The last slice keeps the selection objective honest: only pipelined/pipelined_m are feasible at all (iterative 0/10 and unrolled_k 0/30 feasible, max throughput 11 and 7.14 MSPS), the min-power design is pipelined_m m=3 at 1.75 with 54.3 MSPS, and m has never been searched past 3 while data_width 8..14 has never reached the front - so m=3..8 at small widths is the untested way to push power_index below 1.75."
